WHAT THIS LICENCE COVERS
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

Everything that is not in `tools/` — the reconstructed language itself
and everything derived from it. In this repository that is:

  · frenkisk-dictionary.md — the dictionary
  · frenkisk-kit-translator.md — the translator's grammar
  · frenkisk-index.md — the index
  · frenkisk-specimen.md — the specimen texts
  · frenkisk-paper.pdf, STATE.md, README.md

and, outside it, the source registers, the paradigms, the corpus of
texts and the calligraphic figures from which these are generated.

The CODE in `tools/` is under the MIT licence — see LICENSE.

⚠ **Why two licences.** A constructed language is not software. MIT
permits silent appropriation: someone could take the lexicon, extend it,
and give nothing back. The share-alike clause of CC BY-SA guarantees
that a derived language stays open — the only regime consistent with a
project whose whole method consists in publishing its rulings, its
limits and its mistakes.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
⚠⚠ WHY VERSION 4.0, AND NOT ANOTHER
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

**The lexicon is a database**, and under European law a database is
protected twice over: by copyright in its contents, and by a separate
**sui generis right of the maker** (Directive 96/9/EC) which protects
the investment made in collecting it, independently of any originality.
That second right covers precisely what this project is: 1746 lemmas
gathered, checked and dated.

**CC BY-SA 4.0 is the first version that explicitly licenses that sui
generis right** (§4). A 3.0 would have left it outside the grant — the
licence would have said "reuse this" while an unlicensed right said the
opposite.

